Subject: render: a draw's facts and its stream apart, and no allocation between them

encodeDraw was one function: every refusal, then every word. It is now two halves, the stream unchanged word for word
(the golden streams, both chips):

- drawFacts judges the draw, in the order the refusals always came, and leaves what the stream is made of: what the
  program and the pass decide between them (ProgramFacts) and what this draw's bindings and numbers add (DrawFacts).
- emitDraw writes the stream from the facts, group by group in the one order a draw has, and only the groups asked
  for (kG...). render::encode and render::encodeInPass ask for all of them; CommandBuffer::draw will ask for the few
  that changed. It is a template over the stream it writes: a pm4::Stream now, a pass's stream through its register
  shadow next.

On the way, what a draw allocated for nothing:
- the copy of the fragment stage made to clear the colour registers of outputs without an attachment (1.4 KB moved a
  draw): the registers alone are kept apart, in the facts;
- a vector for each stage's pushed ranges and one for each direct range's words, a vector for each stage's UBO
  descriptors, one for a load's words (pm4::Stream::open: the load's words are written where they lie), one for the
  immediates' padding, two for the vertex decode tables, one for the fragment outputs: all in place now;
- std::strings of the stage's name on entering vertexFetch and stageTextures, and three an attachment in checkTarget:
  made for a refusal alone;
- StageTextures::used and GfxIbos::used, vectors nobody read.


